From 661694f9375b0df185bf38cf917d8e644dd95fab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:00:20 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 24/31] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: enable the media engines on
 every board

The video encoder (ave0), the first ProRes engine (apr0) and both JPEG
engines were enabled only on the J714s, and the video decoder (avd0) only
on the J714c, J714s, J775d and K116s. Every T6050 board's ADT has these
engines at the same addresses, with the same interrupts, DART streams and
power states, so describe their enables once.

Add t6050-media.dtsi with the J714s enables for the decoder, encoder,
ProRes and JPEG engines, their DARTs and their power states, and include it
from every T6050 board after t6050-peripherals.dtsi. The J714s, J714c and
K116s lose their copies of these blocks. The J775d keeps the decoders that
t6050-ultra.dtsi describes enabled; the shared file leaves them alone there.

Every DART enabled here already carries its engine's power state
(dart-ave0 both instances: VENC0_SYS, dart-apr0: SCODEC, dart-jpeg0: JPG,
dart-avd0: AVD_SYS). The encoder's firmware preload is handed over by the
boot loader by compatible, so it needs no per-board change.

The second encoder (ave1) and the second ProRes engine (apr1) of the M5
Max stay disabled: the boot loader hands over only the first encoder's
preloaded firmware, which the AVE driver requires, and the ProRes driver's
T6050 variant only describes the first engine's window layout.

DTBs: the J714s is byte-identical. The J714c gains apple,always-on on
FAB1_SOC, as the J714s, K116s and J775d already have. Every other board
gains whichever of these engines it lacked, so all eleven now enable the
same media nodes as the J714s.

The J714s blocks were written by DjDeveloperr, the J714c decoder block by
Eduard Moldovan and the K116s decoder block by Jens Axboe.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>

[re-cut onto the K114s r3d tree]
